        <article class="prose">
          <section aria-labelledby="start-with-year">
            <h2 id="start-with-year">Start with the year you want to work</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              We start a therapy-fee calculation with the whole practice year.
              Your rate needs to support the sessions you deliver, the notes and
              consultation calls between them, supervision, time off, and the
              weeks your calendar has gaps.
            </p>
            <p>
              We’ll work through that calculation using three inputs: the income
              you want to take home, your practice expenses, and the sessions
              you expect to complete and collect payment for. You can then try
              the same calculation with your own numbers.
            </p>
            <p>
              We use the result as a planning target. Client affordability,
              local demand, your service mix, and payer contracts still affect
              the fee you choose. Our examples are hypothetical and illustrate
              the math; they do not establish a typical therapist’s earnings or
              the right fee for your practice.
            </p>
          </section>
          <aside class="article-tool-callout" aria-label="Try the free tool">
            <span class="tool-icon">
              <svg
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.3"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                <rect x="5" y="3" width="14" height="18" rx="2" />
                <path d="M8 7h8M8 11h2m4 0h2M8 15h2m4 0h2M8 18h2m4 0h2" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            <div>
              <span class="eyebrow">Put this guide to work</span>
              <h3>Put your own numbers to work.</h3>
              <p>
                Use your income goal, expenses, and available sessions to
                calculate a planning rate.
              </p>
              <a class="button" href="/tools/session-rate-calculator/">
                Calculate your session rate <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
              </a>
              <a
                class="article-tool-related"
                href="/tools/good-faith-estimate/"
              >
                Prepare a Good Faith Estimate ↗
              </a>
              <span class="micro">Free to use. No signup required.</span>
            </div>
          </aside>
          <section aria-labelledby="income-expenses">
            <h2 id="income-expenses">Separate income from practice expenses</h2>
            <p>
              Our first step is to list annual expenses: rent or office costs,
              software, professional liability coverage, licensing, continuing
              education, supervision, bookkeeping, and payment processing.
              Separate recurring expenses from one-time startup costs so you can
              see what changes after year one.
            </p>
            <p>
              Our calculator treats the tax reserve as a percentage of income
              remaining after practice expenses. With a $75,000 take-home goal
              and a 25% reserve, you need $100,000 before that reserve. Add
              $20,000 of annual practice expenses and the model needs $120,000
              in collected revenue.
            </p>
            <div class="callout">
              <span class="tag">The revenue calculation</span>
              <p>
                <strong>
                  Annual collected revenue needed = take-home goal ÷ (1 − tax
                  reserve) + practice expenses.
                </strong>
              </p>
            </div>
            <p>
              We use a simplified tax reserve in this model. Your accountant can
              help you choose an appropriate percentage for your situation. If
              you already have a pre-tax income goal, enter that goal and set
              the reserve to zero rather than accounting for taxes twice.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section aria-labelledby="realistic-capacity">
            <h2 id="realistic-capacity">
              Use the sessions you expect to complete
            </h2>
            <p>
              A calendar with 20 weekly openings does not automatically produce
              20 paid sessions. Estimate your working weeks, the share of
              openings that become completed sessions, and the share of billed
              revenue you collect.
            </p>
            <p>
              We keep those last two assumptions separate. A cancellation that
              leaves an unpaid opening belongs in the completed-session rate. An
              unpaid bill after a completed session affects collections.
              Counting the same loss in both will distort your target.
            </p>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Example assumption</th>
                    <th>Value</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Working weeks</td>
                    <td>48</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Offered sessions each week</td>
                    <td>20</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Completed-session rate</td>
                    <td>80%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Collection rate</td>
                    <td>90%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Collection-adjusted sessions</td>
                    <td>691.2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>$120,000 ÷ 691.2</td>
                    <td>$173.61 per session</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p>
              We get 691.2 by multiplying 48 × 20 × 0.80 × 0.90. That decimal is
              a collection-adjusted planning equivalent, which lets us divide
              the annual revenue goal by expected capacity. Your posted fee can
              use a practical rounded number after you review the assumptions.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section aria-labelledby="stress-test">
            <h2 id="stress-test">Test a slower month and a sliding scale</h2>
            <p>
              Next, we test a slower schedule. Lowering the completed-session
              rate to 65%, with the other inputs unchanged, raises the target to
              $213.68. That difference shows how much your fee depends on
              demand. A new practice can plan a cash reserve or a gradual
              caseload ramp rather than expecting a full calendar immediately.
            </p>
            <p>
              For sliding-scale sessions, we use a weighted average. Sixteen
              sessions at $180 and four at $100 average $164 across those 20
              sessions. Compare that average with your target. Change the number
              of reduced-fee openings, overhead, or income goal until the plan
              is workable.
            </p>
            <p>
              Protect time for clinical work outside the appointment and the
              operations needed to run the practice. Increasing session capacity
              may also increase your documentation load, supervision needs, or
              other costs. Revisit the inputs once you have actual booking and
              collection data.
            </p>
          </section>
          <section aria-labelledby="explain-fees">
            <h2 id="explain-fees">Make the fee conversation specific</h2>
            <p>
              We recommend making the fee conversation specific: explain your
              session fee, payment timing, cancellation policy, and whether you
              provide a superbill. Explain how any reduced-fee arrangement works
              and when you will review it. Put the agreed terms in the
              appropriate practice documents.
            </p>
            <p>
              For clients who are uninsured or not using insurance, review the{" "}
              <a href="/blog/good-faith-estimate-for-therapy/">
                Good Faith Estimate requirements
              </a>
              . If a client plans to seek out-of-network reimbursement, use the{" "}
              <a href="/blog/superbill-for-therapy/">superbill guide</a> and ask
              them to verify their own benefits.
            </p>
            <p>
              Enter your assumptions in the{" "}
              <a href="/tools/session-rate-calculator/">
                session rate calculator
              </a>
              , save the result, and set a date to compare it with your first
              three months of completed sessions and collections.
            </p>
          </section>
